<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deir al-Qamar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Deir el Qamar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deir al-Qamar is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. It was the seat of the Ma'n and Shihab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> until Bashir II moved his court to nearby Beiteddine, and it preserves the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Christian population suffered a massacre in the civil war of 1860, and many of its old stone houses and palaces survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-al-qamar.jpg" alt="Deir al-Qamar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrice Bon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deir_El-Qamar_Minist%C3%A8re_du_tourisme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
